# store-bounds.manifest — every append-only store that grows without a bound, and the cap it must
# stay under. Read by scripts/store-bounds-census.sh (M9c, MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2.md §11.3).
#
# WHY A MANIFEST AND NOT EIGHT FIXES. §8.5.5 named eight unbounded stores. Fixing them one at a time
# leaves the CLASS alive: the ninth store ships next week with nobody watching it, because nothing in
# the repo knows that "a store must have a bound" is a rule. A declared cap per store turns eight spot
# fixes into one mechanism plus eight one-line rows, and adding a row is the cheapest possible way to
# put a new store under watch.
#
# THE CENSUS NEVER DELETES, ROTATES, OR TRUNCATES. It measures, compares, and PAGES. Deletion is the
# destination's property, never the census's decision (memory append-only-store-safety-rules — an
# `mv -f` "archive" destroyed 1,461 lines). Each row therefore carries the REMEDY as text for the
# owner to run, not a command this tool will execute.
#
# FORMAT — one store per line, four `|`-separated fields; `#` comments and blank lines ignored:
#
#   <glob>|<max_mb>|<owner-row>|<remedy>
#
#   glob      Path relative to $HOME/.claude (never absolute, never containing `..`). Ordinary shell
#             glob; it is expanded ONE level as written and never walked recursively — deliberately,
#             because a recursive walk over ~/.claude would traverse the 7,359-file / 4.26 GB
#             transcript corpus under projects/ on every run and become its own load problem.
#             A glob matching several files (a `.bak-*` set) is summed and judged as ONE store.
#   max_mb    Integer cap. A no-match glob sums to 0 and is trivially under cap — an absent store is
#             genuinely not a growth problem.
#   owner-row The MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2 row that owns the remedy, or `-` when unowned. The census does
#             not fix anything, so a bound without a named owner is a page nobody can action.
#   remedy    Free text (may contain `|`), shown verbatim on the page.
#
# CALIBRATION — CAPS ARE A RATCHET, NOT A FORECAST. Each cap is set just above the store's measured
# size at 2026-07-30 so that GROWTH pages while today's steady state stays quiet. The two exceptions
# are deliberate and expected to page on the very first live run (AC20 — a ratchet that cannot
# demonstrate a catch is indistinguishable from one that is broken):
#   · logs/bash-execution.log        measured 36 MB against a 24 MB cap
#   · state/session-index.db.bak-*   measured ~49 MB against a 1 MB cap
# Raising a cap is a decision with a reason, made in a commit — never a silent edit to quiet a page.

logs/bash-execution.log|24|6|rotate: gzip+truncate per its own header
logs/bash-commands.log|48|6|same
autonomy/idl.jsonl|32|10|archive-compact per idl chain
logs/teammate-checkpoint.log|24|6|rotate
session-index.db|96|9|retention backlogged b0d889846885
state/session-index.db.bak-*|1|9|stale backup — delete candidate, operator call
logs/cc-reaper.log|16|4|rotate
history.jsonl|16|-|rotate
logs/auth-timeseries.jsonl|32|-|rotate: already in rotate-autonomy-logs.sh DEFAULT_TARGETS (25 MiB, keep 8) — a breach here means the rotator is not running, not that the store is misbehaving
logs/account-utilization.jsonl|32|-|rotate: already in rotate-autonomy-logs.sh DEFAULT_TARGETS (25 MiB, keep 8)
logs/accounts-local.*.log|5|-|append-only diagnostics; truncate with : > ~/.claude/logs/accounts-local.out.log (the stamp is the liveness evidence)
# Usage levers (docs/plans/USAGE_LEVERS.md § W7+ §6, wave S6; 2026-10-25). These stores feed 28-d
# scoring windows, so their bound is a RETENTION, not a ratchet on today's size: each glob takes the
# live file plus its rotations, which scripts/rotate-autonomy-logs.sh deletes only past 42 d
# (never under 35). Caps are 42 d at the measured rate with headroom: subagent-long-bash ~1.2 MB/d,
# idle-keepalive ~0.85 MB/d, the rest well under 0.3 MB/d. lever-ledger is bounded by its writer
# (cc-lever-score write_ledger), the per-session state dirs by the rotator's mtime prune.
logs/lever-arms.jsonl*|32|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass (64 MiB gate, age-retained 42 d); a breach means the rotator is not running
logs/lever-flags.jsonl*|4|-|same; one audit row per cc-lever write — a breach means a writer is looping
logs/lever-ledger.jsonl|32|-|cc-lever-score write_ledger drops rows past CC_LEVER_LEDGER_RETAIN_D (42 d); a breach means the hourly job stopped writing or the floor moved
logs/lever-score.jsonl*|16|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
logs/lever-guard.jsonl*|16|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass; ~145 tick rows/day from cc-lever-guard plus the hourly guard rows
logs/lever-judge.jsonl*|16|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
logs/lever-rounds.jsonl*|8|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
logs/idle-keepalive.jsonl*|64|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
logs/idle-recycle-shadow.jsonl*|16|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
logs/subagent-long-bash.jsonl*|96|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
logs/workflow-lean-shadow.jsonl*|16|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh lever pass
state/levers/arms/*|8|-|rotate-autonomy-logs.sh prunes files past 42 d by mtime
state/levers/proc/*|8|-|same
state/levers/lineage/*|4|-|same
state/levers/d4-pending/*|8|-|same
